    "path": "/guides/rottweiler-grooming",
    "type": "guide",
    "title": "Rottweiler Grooming Guide | UnSaid Memories",
    "metaDescription": "Rottweiler grooming: double-coat shedding care, bath schedule, big-dog nail tips. Memphis groomer guide for Rottweiler parents.",
    "h1": "Rottweiler Grooming Guide",
    "copyParagraphs": [
      "Rottweilers look like they were carved from granite, and their grooming is refreshingly straightforward for such a powerful dog: a short double coat, moderate shedding, and a routine any owner can handle. The real grooming skill with a Rottie isn't technique — it's trust. Here's how our Memphis groomers keep Rottweilers handsome and cooperative at UnSaid Memories.",
      "A Rottweiler wears a short, dense double coat — straight, coarse outer hair over a softer undercoat — that sheds moderately year-round with heavier seasonal turnover. It's more coat than it looks: the undercoat is what ends up on your furniture. Never shave a Rottweiler; the double coat regulates temperature and protects the skin, and the breed's black coat absorbs serious Memphis sun. The classic black-and-tan markings look sharpest on a well-brushed, clean coat — this is a breed where basic maintenance pays visible dividends.",
      "Two to three times a week with a rubber curry or slicker, plus an undercoat rake during spring and fall when the undercoat turns over. Work with the grain in firm strokes — Rottweilers are sturdy dogs that appreciate a confident hand, not tentative pats. The neck, chest, and britches hold the most undercoat. A rubber curry during bath time doubles as a deshed session. Keep sessions matter-of-fact and treat-rewarded; Rottweilers respect routine and calm consistency.",
      "Every six to eight weeks, or when dirty. Rottweilers are clean dogs by nature, and overbathing strips the coat's natural weather resistance. Wet thoroughly — the dense coat resists water — shampoo, and rinse longer than you think necessary; trapped soap under a double coat causes itching. Dry completely, especially in the skin folds of the neck where moisture hides. Rottweilers can be suspicious of new experiences, so introduce the tub and dryer gradually with a dog that's new to professional grooming — calm confidence from the handler does most of the work.",
      "Nails every three to four weeks, and take them seriously: Rottweiler nails are thick, strong, and often black, which hides the quick. If you're trimming at home, take small amounts and have styptic powder ready; if you're unsure, book a pro — there's no prize for guessing wrong on a thick black nail. Many Rotties wear their nails down on pavement, but dewclaws never touch ground, so check those every time. A Dremel-style grinder is excellent for gradual shortening once the dog accepts the vibration. Long nails on a heavy dog change the gait and stress the joints — this matters more at 100+ pounds.",
      "Ears: medium, floppy, with decent airflow — check weekly and clean with a vet-approved solution as needed. Teeth: brush a few times a week and ask your vet about dental cleanings; Rottweilers are powerful chewers, which helps, but tartar still accumulates. The temperament note: many Rottweilers are aloof with strangers, and grooming requires a stranger to handle their feet, ears, and tail. Build the relationship deliberately — desensitization visits with treats, a regular groomer your dog knows, and handling practice at home. A Rottweiler that trusts its groomer is a dream on the table; one that doesn't is 110 pounds of \"no.\"",
      "| When | What | |---|---| | 2–3x per week | Brush with curry or slicker | | Every 3–4 weeks | Nail trim | | Every 6–8 weeks | Bath, thorough dry | | Spring & fall | Add undercoat rake sessions; book a deshed | | Ongoing | Teeth brushing; weekly ear checks |",
      "Book a bath and deshed every couple of months — the high-velocity dryer does in one visit what takes weeks of home brushing. More importantly, book with a groomer experienced in confident handling of powerful breeds, and keep the same groomer so trust builds visit to visit. If your Rottweiler is genuinely fearful of grooming, invest in short desensitization appointments before attempting a full groom — patience now prevents a lifetime of stressful appointments."
    ],
