    "path": "/policies/matted-coats",
    "type": "landing",
    "title": "Matted Coat Policy | UnSaid Memories Memphis",
    "metaDescription": "How we handle matted coats: dematting vs shave-down, additional fees, and how to prevent matting.",
    "h1": "Matted Coat Policy",
    "copyParagraphs": [
      "Mats are tangled, felted clumps of fur that tighten against the skin. They trap moisture, hide skin irritation, and pull painfully with every movement. Light tangles are normal; tight matting is a welfare issue — and it changes what we can safely do in a groom.",
      "For light to moderate tangles, we can carefully demat — working mats out with specialized tools, patience, and breaks. This takes significant extra time and is priced accordingly. We'll assess at check-in and quote the additional time before starting.",
      "For severe matting tight against the skin, dematting is painful and risks cutting or tearing skin. In these cases we will not brush it out. Instead, we recommend a humane shave-down: clipping the coat short with a surgical-length blade to remove the mats safely and give the skin a fresh start. It's not the prettiest option — it's the kindest one.",
      "If we find matting at check-in that changes the service plan, we stop and talk to you before proceeding. You'll know the options, the price difference, and our recommendation. Nothing changes without your okay.",
      "Significant dematting work is billed for the extra time involved — your groomer quotes it at check-in. A humane shave-down is priced as a haircut service; regrowth trims afterward return to standard pricing.",
      "The fix is routine: brush long or curly coats several times a week (down to the skin, not just the top layer), keep a regular grooming schedule — Bath Club memberships exist partly for this — and ask us to show you the right brush technique for your pet's coat. Our guide on matting prevention covers it in detail."
    ],
