      "Cats are trainable — they just negotiate. Our training and behavior department stocks the tools that make good habits easy: clickers and training kits for teaching sit, come, and carrier-loading; deterrent sprays and mats that make counters boring and couches unappealing; calming products for the anxious, the newly adopted, and the multi-cat household finding its hierarchy; and enrichment puzzles that give busy brains a job. The principles are simple: reward what you want, manage the environment for what you don't, and never punish — fear breaks trust faster than any behavior improves. Counter surfing? Provide a legal high perch and make the counter unrewarding. Scratching the couch? That's a furniture and scratchers problem, not a training problem — give them something better first. New cat in the house? Slow introductions across days, with scent-swapping before face-to-face. Pair every training plan with high-value Cat Treats — the currency of cooperation. Our staff has talked plenty of Memphis cat parents through the classics; stop by 699 Waring Rd and tell us what your cat is doing. Short sessions win: two to three minutes, a few times a day, ending on a success. Teach practical behaviors first — coming when called, going into the carrier on cue, and accepting handling — because those pay off at the vet. Use the highest-value treats for the hardest asks and everyday treats for easy wins. If a behavior stalls, make the training easier rather than the reward bigger; cats quit when the puzzle feels unfair. Patience measured in weeks, not days, is the whole method. We've heard it before."
